// Replaces passing_two_fields_of_same_struct_by_mixed_reference_in_same_
// call_conflicts, which asserted this exact program is ill-formed and
// cited no clause for it. Two clauses say otherwise, and both scope
// reborrowing to an *existing reference or span binding* -- of which this
// function has none:
//
//   ch02 §6.2(7): "If a local variable or parameter of reference type,
//   `std::span<T>`, or `std::span<const T>` is used to initialize another
//   local variable of reference or span type, or to satisfy a parameter
//   of reference or span type, the new binding is a reborrow if it
//   aliases the same underlying object or range through that existing
//   binding."
//
//   ch02 §6.6(10): "a function parameter of type `const T&` binds
//   directly to the argument's designated object under the ordinary
//   rules for reference binding. If that direct binding aliases an
//   already-existing reference or span binding, it is a reborrow
//   governed by §6.2(7)-(10)."
//
// `p` is a plain local struct, not a reference or span binding, so
// neither `p.x` nor `p.y` is a reborrow of anything, and §6.2(9.2)'s
// "further reborrow formed from that existing binding" has no existing
// binding to be formed from. `p.x` and `p.y` are also distinct objects
// ([intro.object]/1: "A subobject is an object"), so they alias nothing
// of each other's.
//
// borrow_whole_struct_conflicts_with_borrow_of_its_field, next door, is
// the case §6.2(7) *does* reach -- it binds `Point& whole = p;` first --
// and it still fails.
struct Point {
    int x;
    int y;
};

int add_into(int& dst, const int& src) {
    dst = dst + src;
    return 0;
}

int f() {
    Point p{};
    p.x = 1;
    p.y = 2;
    add_into(p.x, p.y);
    return p.x;
}
